## Wiki Maintenance (Proactive)

You maintain a persistent knowledge wiki in Obsidian at the path in `WIKI_VAULT`. This happens as part of your natural workflow, not only when `/wiki` is invoked. The wiki captures knowledge that would otherwise vanish with the conversation.

### When to Write

- A planning session produces a concrete plan or PRD
- An architecture or design decision is made (especially with tradeoffs)
- A spike or exploration is completed (whether it succeeded or was abandoned)
- A brainstorming session produces ideas worth remembering
- A concept is discussed in depth for the first time
- Scope is defined with rationale

### When NOT to Write

- Routine bug fixes or refactors (unless the root cause is surprising)
- Debugging steps (unless the finding is non-obvious)
- Session transcripts or play-by-play narration
- Summaries of what was just done
- File listings, command outputs, raw data
- Anything obvious from reading the code

### Where Pages Go

Determine project slug: `basename $(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)` in kebab-case.

```
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/devlog.md      — append dated entries
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/plans/         — PRDs, specs
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/decisions/     — ADRs
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/spikes/        — explorations
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/projects/<slug>/concepts/      — project-specific concepts
$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/concepts/                      — cross-project concepts
```

Create the project directory and subdirectories if they don't exist.

### Page Formats

**Devlog** (append to existing file, newest entry first):
```
## [YYYY-MM-DD] Brief title
- Key outcome or decision
- Link to [[decision]] or [[plan]] page if created
```

**Decision** (new file per decision):
```
---
type: decision
status: accepted
created: YYYY-MM-DD
project: <slug>
---
# Title
## Context — ## Decision — ## Consequences — ## Alternatives Considered
```

**Plan** (new file per plan):
```
---
type: plan
status: active
created: YYYY-MM-DD
project: <slug>
---
# Title
## Goal — ## Background — ## Approach — ## Scope — ## Open Questions
```

**Spike** (new file per exploration):
```
---
type: spike
outcome: success|abandoned|partial
created: YYYY-MM-DD
project: <slug>
---
# Title
## Question — ## What Was Tried — ## Findings — ## Conclusion
```

### Anti-Bloat Rules

- No "In this session we..." framing
- No timestamps in prose (frontmatter only)
- No attribution to "Claude" or "the AI"
- No hedging ("it might be worth considering...")
- No listing things obvious from the code
- Maximum 2 paragraphs per section
- Prefer lists for facts and tradeoffs
- Write as a knowledgeable colleague documenting for future reference

### After Writing

Update `$WIKI_VAULT/wiki/index.md` — this is mandatory with no exceptions. Every session that writes any wiki page must update the index. Add new projects under `## Projects` if they don't exist; add new page entries under the project section. Keep entries to one line with a short description hook.

### Devlog Reading Strategy

The devlog is the first thing to read when starting work on a project. The session-start hook loads the 3 most recent entries automatically. If you need context from earlier sessions, use the Read tool with an offset — do not load the full devlog into context. For large devlogs (projects like `unlearn`, `verdant`, `taxer`, `wardrobe` all exceed 30KB), loading the full file is expensive and unnecessary; scan headings first with a targeted Bash grep to find the relevant date range, then read only that section.
